ArrayList vs LinkedList — when would you choose each?
▼
ArrayList: choose when you need random access, mostly read-heavy workloads, or cache-friendly sequential iteration. Better CPU cache performance due to contiguous memory.

LinkedList: choose when you have frequent insertions/deletions at the head or middle, and you never need random access. Each node has 24-byte overhead (value + prev + next pointers) — memory-heavy.

Gotcha for managers: In practice, ArrayList is almost always faster than LinkedList even for insertions because of CPU cache line efficiency. Benchmark before choosing LinkedList.

